1
00:00:00,702 --> 00:00:07,465
[SPEAKER_00]: This sutta is from chapter 47 of the Sānyūtanikāya, sutta number 8, called The Cook.

2
00:00:11,807 --> 00:00:12,347
[SPEAKER_00]: bhikkhus.

3
00:00:12,647 --> 00:00:20,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose a foolish, incompetent, unskillful cook were to present a king or royal minister with various kinds of curries.

4
00:00:21,011 --> 00:00:26,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Sour, bitter, pungent, sweet, sharp, mild, salty, bland.

5
00:00:27,782 --> 00:00:34,364
[SPEAKER_00]: That foolish, incompetent, unskillful cook does not pick up the sign of his master's own preference.

6
00:00:35,264 --> 00:00:55,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Today, this curry pleased my master, or he reached for this one, or he took a lot of this one, or he spoke in praise of this one, or the sour curry pleased my master, or he reached for the sour one, or he took a lot of the sour one, or he spoke in praise of the sour one, or the bitter curry, the pungent curry, so on and so forth.

7
00:00:56,405 --> 00:00:58,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Or he spoke in praise of some other curry.

8
00:00:59,268 --> 00:01:06,435
[SPEAKER_00]: That foolish, incompetent, unskillful cook does not gain gifts of clothing, wages, and bonuses.

9
00:01:06,995 --> 00:01:07,736
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

10
00:01:08,177 --> 00:01:14,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that foolish, incompetent, unskillful cook does not pick up the sign of his own master's preference.

11
00:01:16,243 --> 00:01:30,990
[SPEAKER_00]: So too, bhikkhus, here some foolish, incompetent, unskillful bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.

12
00:01:31,731 --> 00:01:40,935
[SPEAKER_00]: While he dwells contemplating the body in the body, his mind does not become concentrated, his corruptions are not abandoned, he does not pick up that sign.

13
00:01:41,698 --> 00:01:53,403
[SPEAKER_00]: He dwells contemplating feelings and feelings, mind and mind, phenomena and phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.

14
00:01:54,424 --> 00:01:59,666
[SPEAKER_00]: While he dwells contemplating phenomena and phenomena, his mind does not become concentrated.

15
00:01:59,906 --> 00:02:01,527
[SPEAKER_00]: His corruptions are not abandoned.

16
00:02:01,847 --> 00:02:03,308
[SPEAKER_00]: He does not pick up that sign.

17
00:02:04,834 --> 00:02:13,318
[SPEAKER_00]: That foolish, incompetent, unskillful bhikkhu does not gain pleasant dwellings in this very life, nor does he gain mindfulness and clear comprehension.

18
00:02:13,778 --> 00:02:14,498
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

19
00:02:14,878 --> 00:02:20,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, bhikkhus, that foolish, incompetent, unskillful bhikkhu does not pick up the sign of his own mind.

20
00:02:22,462 --> 00:02:30,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose, bhikkhus, a wise, competent, skillful cook were to present a king or royal minister with various kinds of curries.

21
00:02:31,331 --> 00:02:36,272
[SPEAKER_00]: That wise, competent, skillful cook picks up the sign of his master's own preference.

22
00:02:36,752 --> 00:02:42,934
[SPEAKER_00]: Today, this curry pleased my master, or he spoke in praise of this one, so on and so forth.

23
00:02:43,894 --> 00:02:48,955
[SPEAKER_00]: That wise, competent, skillful cook gains gifts of clothing, wages and bonuses.

24
00:02:49,575 --> 00:02:50,256
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

25
00:02:50,456 --> 00:02:55,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that wise, competent, skillful cook picks up the sign of his own master's preference.

26
00:02:57,055 --> 00:02:57,996
[SPEAKER_00]: So too bhikkhus.

27
00:02:58,476 --> 00:03:04,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, some wise, competent, skillful bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body.

28
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:10,404
[SPEAKER_00]: Ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.

29
00:03:11,744 --> 00:03:15,947
[SPEAKER_00]: While he dwells contemplating the body in the body, his mind becomes concentrated.

30
00:03:16,267 --> 00:03:17,668
[SPEAKER_00]: His corruptions are abandoned.

31
00:03:17,808 --> 00:03:19,009
[SPEAKER_00]: He picks up that sign.

32
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:25,273
[SPEAKER_00]: He does the same with feelings and feelings, mind and mind, phenomena and phenomena.

33
00:03:26,273 --> 00:03:34,176
[SPEAKER_00]: That wise, competent, skillful bhikkhu gains pleasant dwellings in this very life, and he gains mindfulness and clear comprehension.

34
00:03:34,956 --> 00:03:35,676
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

35
00:03:35,876 --> 00:03:41,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Because bhikkhus, that wise, competent, skillful bhikkhu, picks up the sign of his own mind.

36
00:03:43,219 --> 00:03:50,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Some foolish bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body and the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful.

37
00:03:51,962 --> 00:03:55,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it seems like he is, in a sense, practicing satipaṭṭhāna,

38
00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:09,492
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he says, his mind does not become concentrated, his corruptions are not abandoned, he does not pick up that sign.

39
00:04:10,352 --> 00:04:15,194
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how an idea will still, like, remain unfinished, so to speak.

40
00:04:16,554 --> 00:04:20,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, sign in terms of... Nimitta.

41
00:04:20,415 --> 00:04:22,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Signs, characteristics, tokens of a thing.

42
00:04:23,476 --> 00:04:26,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Of the body, or the ceiling.

43
00:04:26,037 --> 00:04:28,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Or whatever phenomena of the mind, in this case.

44
00:04:30,387 --> 00:04:36,249
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see how your mind sort of wants to turn away from it, doesn't want to be focused really, or whatever.

45
00:04:36,669 --> 00:04:37,729
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not discerning.

46
00:04:38,189 --> 00:04:39,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, not discerning that.

47
00:04:39,710 --> 00:04:46,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Not discerning that background of the phenomena that appear as a result of in practicing mindfulness.

48
00:04:49,313 --> 00:04:55,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's why I think it's in that similar sort of... well, in that principle, I think

49
00:05:09,042 --> 00:05:10,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't that supposed to be better?

50
00:05:10,842 --> 00:05:11,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

51
00:05:11,883 --> 00:05:14,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, how you can actually benefit from it?

52
00:05:14,723 --> 00:05:21,784
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do it, usually there's almost like an exercise, in like, okay, you're not going to go with your will, you go with the senior monk's will.

53
00:05:22,445 --> 00:05:29,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because of some blind devotion, simply because, well, that's just another point of view to your experience as a whole that has arisen.

54
00:05:29,726 --> 00:05:34,667
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a point of view that's the closest to me, my point of view, there's a point of view of another person.

55
00:05:36,651 --> 00:05:43,974
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... that exercise can sort of train the mind in picking up on things or signs, eh... nimittas.

56
00:05:45,875 --> 00:06:00,522
[SPEAKER_01]: In this particular case of what a senior monk might need or require, but, eh... generally, it will actually train the mind to pick on the signs, eh... regardless whether it's internal or external, whether it's your point of view or their point of view.

57
00:06:00,702 --> 00:06:01,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

58
00:06:01,522 --> 00:06:03,443
[SPEAKER_00]: You will start seeing more of the background.

59
00:06:04,452 --> 00:06:21,460
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you know, ah, you're not just doing what you want to do, you're actually following, in a way, someone else's will, based on trust, based on confidence, uh, and then you start picking on these things, then the mind just starts doing the same everywhere, uh, against its will, so to speak.

60
00:06:23,921 --> 00:06:28,963
[SPEAKER_01]: In that sense, um, even such sort of, um, a task of attending

61
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:45,228
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that simile is quite close to that, like, you know, he would, you know, he wouldn't have to.

62
00:06:45,328 --> 00:06:51,233
[SPEAKER_01]: His job is to cook, but he really kind of makes that extra effort, because that extra effort is not actually effort as much.

63
00:06:51,333 --> 00:06:53,735
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like just learning how to notice things, always.

64
00:06:54,475 --> 00:06:57,017
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter if it's internal or external, or both.

65
00:06:58,959 --> 00:07:01,080
[SPEAKER_01]: You start picking on stuff.

66
00:07:02,181 --> 00:07:04,183
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is a truth in that, like, when

67
00:07:05,732 --> 00:07:18,019
[SPEAKER_01]: When you meet monks throughout the monastery, eh, the ones that sort of pick on little things, like, what, kind of, oh, what needs to be, like, they don't need to be taught, you know, constantly do this or do that, and they're just like a robot.

68
00:07:18,479 --> 00:07:19,660
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll be picking on stuff.

69
00:07:20,320 --> 00:07:23,862
[SPEAKER_01]: They are generally more aware, because that's the way how you pick on it.

70
00:07:34,667 --> 00:07:36,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do not neglect that habit.

71
00:07:37,108 --> 00:07:42,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a place for that whole kind of Thai training and stuff, but obviously, you know, some people just kind of take it blindly.

72
00:07:43,692 --> 00:07:46,413
[SPEAKER_01]: People don't necessarily benefit from it as much as they could.

73
00:07:48,274 --> 00:07:49,175
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh...

74
00:07:50,376 --> 00:07:52,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it's quite a... it's a good practice.

75
00:07:53,377 --> 00:07:54,078
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a good practice.

76
00:07:54,258 --> 00:07:57,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And... because, you know, those are easy things to pick on.

77
00:07:58,060 --> 00:07:59,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he lies empty.

78
00:07:59,421 --> 00:08:01,242
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't use this umbrella.

79
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:20,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, subtle tokens of your mind, like, how it, like, to what degree turns away from, you know, practicing concentration on a heavy, humid day that doesn't want to do and so on, like, that will take time to discern.

80
00:08:20,124 --> 00:08:28,366
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're constantly just trying to discern only that, it would be like, basically, what's the expression?

81
00:08:29,787 --> 00:08:31,147
[SPEAKER_01]: A piece is too big to chew.

82
00:08:32,135 --> 00:08:34,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Right away, it all disappears in a big blob.

83
00:08:35,177 --> 00:08:36,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

84
00:08:36,097 --> 00:08:37,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it just gets overwhelming.

85
00:08:38,759 --> 00:08:46,003
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same principle that you can train in regards to the external circumstances, and then it becomes easier to be seen.

86
00:08:49,284 --> 00:08:52,786
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when it's talking about signs and tokens, it's a very kind of general term.

87
00:08:53,086 --> 00:08:54,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Very general term.

88
00:08:54,787 --> 00:08:57,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Just basically how you know anything is anything.

89
00:08:57,209 --> 00:08:58,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

90
00:08:58,829 --> 00:09:01,391
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, you know, that's how the whole kind of the

91
00:09:13,229 --> 00:09:19,653
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing that you have signs of, that defines it as such, that designates it as such.

92
00:09:20,033 --> 00:09:22,995
[SPEAKER_01]: So, signs and tokens with the level of intentionality as well.

93
00:09:23,255 --> 00:09:27,418
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's not, it's not kind of, there's not like a clear distinction.

94
00:09:27,878 --> 00:09:29,139
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the level of the mind.

95
00:09:29,499 --> 00:09:30,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, on that background.

96
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:34,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just by picking sign and tokens with your eyes, your ears,

97
00:09:35,306 --> 00:09:38,768
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already training your mind in regard to the same principle.

98
00:09:39,909 --> 00:09:45,932
[SPEAKER_01]: As opposed to just kind of going straight into the abstract mode and trying to sign it open and then like you have nothing to root it in.

99
00:09:47,013 --> 00:09:56,259
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you start seeing it in an obvious course manner, you can start sort of extrapolating the principle out of it and then discerning it elsewhere.

100
00:09:56,479 --> 00:09:59,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Even just in the purely, you know, on a level of thoughts.

101
00:10:01,042 --> 00:10:01,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

102
00:10:02,795 --> 00:10:07,718
[SPEAKER_00]: I just thought about that at that time with the sign of the mind, which is something you tend to bring up.

103
00:10:08,679 --> 00:10:09,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, basically, that's the question.

104
00:10:09,900 --> 00:10:12,261
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I know my mind is in such a way?

105
00:10:13,062 --> 00:10:14,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, pick up a token of it.

106
00:10:15,123 --> 00:10:16,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what do you mean in such a way?

107
00:10:16,925 --> 00:10:17,445
[SPEAKER_00]: In what way?

108
00:10:17,805 --> 00:10:20,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, I have a sleepy mind.

109
00:10:20,887 --> 00:10:21,848
[SPEAKER_00]: But how is that?

110
00:10:21,928 --> 00:10:23,009
[SPEAKER_00]: How is it that I know that?

111
00:10:23,569 --> 00:10:24,250
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you know that?

112
00:10:24,310 --> 00:10:25,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because the sleepiness is there.

113
00:10:26,971 --> 00:10:29,813
[SPEAKER_01]: But how can you know that that is the sign of the mind?

114
00:10:30,254 --> 00:10:32,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you'll know that when you know it's a sign of the mind.

115
00:10:37,002 --> 00:10:46,707
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't mean to say, like, oh, I need things in front of me.

116
00:10:46,747 --> 00:10:48,188
[SPEAKER_00]: There's the sleepiness there.

117
00:10:48,228 --> 00:10:51,790
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, like, just as a rhetorical question I asked myself.

118
00:10:52,431 --> 00:10:52,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I see.

119
00:10:53,111 --> 00:10:55,192
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought it was an actual question.

120
00:10:55,212 --> 00:10:57,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how do you explain that?

121
00:10:58,354 --> 00:10:59,354
[SPEAKER_01]: I see what you mean.

122
00:10:59,434 --> 00:10:59,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

123
00:11:00,015 --> 00:11:01,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I know exactly.

124
00:11:01,235 --> 00:11:01,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Start looking.

125
00:11:01,936 --> 00:11:02,356
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll do it.

126
00:11:04,963 --> 00:11:06,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I'm restless or I'm sleepy.

127
00:11:07,905 --> 00:11:08,865
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

128
00:11:08,885 --> 00:11:09,666
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I know that?

129
00:11:09,726 --> 00:11:16,250
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you start looking for certain tokens and signs of restlessness or sleepiness that sort of define the state of the mind.

130
00:11:16,750 --> 00:11:23,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you become proficient in that, so to speak, in seeing that phenomenon, that peripheral, then that's the sign of the mind.

131
00:11:23,174 --> 00:11:24,415
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize the peripheral.

132
00:11:25,536 --> 00:11:27,617
[SPEAKER_01]: The phenomenon of the peripheral is what the mind is.

133
00:11:28,117 --> 00:11:29,058
[SPEAKER_01]: It is that peripheral.

134
00:11:30,059 --> 00:11:31,359
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not seen as a phenomenon.

135
00:11:33,467 --> 00:11:37,688
[SPEAKER_01]: You might see peripheral in regard to what's actual, but you don't see peripheral as a phenomenon of peripheral.

136
00:11:38,369 --> 00:11:39,209
[SPEAKER_01]: On the peripheral.

137
00:11:40,369 --> 00:11:42,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you don't see the sign of your mind.

138
00:11:42,130 --> 00:11:43,650
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see your mind as a phenomenon.

139
00:11:45,791 --> 00:11:46,291
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean?

140
00:11:46,391 --> 00:11:48,252
[SPEAKER_01]: The peripheral of the peripheral?

141
00:11:48,272 --> 00:11:48,612
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

142
00:11:48,632 --> 00:11:49,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Peripheral on the peripheral.

143
00:11:50,432 --> 00:11:51,213
[SPEAKER_01]: On the peripheral.

144
00:11:52,193 --> 00:11:52,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

145
00:11:52,413 --> 00:11:57,294
[SPEAKER_01]: You mean peripheral of the peripheral is not necessarily, but you see peripheral as peripheral.

146
00:11:57,695 --> 00:11:57,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh.

147
00:11:58,535 --> 00:12:02,296
[SPEAKER_01]: As a phenomenon that always arises in regard to no matter what actual.

148
00:12:03,134 --> 00:12:05,015
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it stops being, kind of, dependent.

149
00:12:05,615 --> 00:12:12,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Your inference of it stops being dependent upon the content that you're actual, and then I see the peripheral.

150
00:12:13,019 --> 00:12:17,421
[SPEAKER_01]: You start like that, but the point is, basically, discern the nature of peripheral.

151
00:12:17,801 --> 00:12:19,982
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's basically discerning the phenomenon of the mind.

152
00:12:23,064 --> 00:12:32,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Where, for you, you know, at that stage, you would even stop, kind of, having to think about peripheral or actual, which is always, kind of, a relation there, but you see, that's what the mind is.

153
00:12:35,874 --> 00:12:49,048
[SPEAKER_01]: And obviously, learning to see as peripheral, like seeing peripheral on the peripheral, I mean, like, you don't over-attend it and think of it in abstract, actual terms.

154
00:12:51,231 --> 00:12:52,252
[SPEAKER_01]: But you see it for what it is.

155
00:13:10,364 --> 00:13:18,327
[SPEAKER_00]: And one should also, I guess, see its determinations, this peripheral side of the mind, like what determined it.

156
00:13:19,628 --> 00:13:23,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, I mean, you'll be seeing those only once you start seeing the peripheral side.

157
00:13:23,750 --> 00:13:26,871
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be seeing determinations and then reveal what they determine.

158
00:13:27,431 --> 00:13:30,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Determinations can only be seen once you have that which is being determined.

159
00:13:30,713 --> 00:13:32,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So through that, you see that which determines it.

160
00:13:34,154 --> 00:13:35,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not a linear connection.

161
00:13:36,973 --> 00:13:40,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the determination left determines this thing far, far right.

162
00:13:42,038 --> 00:13:50,607
[SPEAKER_01]: It's by seeing the determinant through there, through it, through its simultaneous presence, you can discern what determines it simultaneously while it's present.

163
00:13:53,010 --> 00:13:54,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is feeling and perception.

164
00:13:55,172 --> 00:13:56,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, if you're talking about the mind, yeah.

165
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:33,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a difference between just a mind that is, let's say, sleeping, versus being affected by the hindrance of sleepiness, thought, and torpor, however you want to translate it?

166
00:14:35,413 --> 00:14:35,913
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean?

167
00:14:36,313 --> 00:14:38,695
[SPEAKER_01]: You mean, can Adam be sleeping?

168
00:14:38,735 --> 00:14:40,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, of course, they can become tired.

169
00:14:40,676 --> 00:14:40,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

170
00:14:40,917 --> 00:14:42,138
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's different for them.

171
00:14:42,678 --> 00:14:46,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because their mind is not bound by any of such hindrances.

172
00:14:47,918 --> 00:14:48,738
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be violent.

173
00:14:50,039 --> 00:14:53,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Those hindrances won't be inducing any wrong views or any blurts.

174
00:14:54,261 --> 00:14:56,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Put in mind that I'm a robot free from hindrances.

175
00:14:56,902 --> 00:14:58,623
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, it's not a hindrance anymore.

176
00:15:01,124 --> 00:15:04,966
[SPEAKER_00]: But they'd still be described in a sense as though they could say, I am tired.

177
00:15:06,026 --> 00:15:07,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, of course, yeah.

178
00:15:08,067 --> 00:15:14,130
[SPEAKER_01]: But then it's when they say, I am, it becomes just a hollow shell of expression that we spoke before.

179
00:15:15,697 --> 00:15:19,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas, for a patugin who's tired, that just means lack of mindfulness, basically.

180
00:15:19,719 --> 00:15:20,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

181
00:15:20,900 --> 00:15:35,267
